00:01:03.755 --> 00:01:07.295
Hi. I'm Joe Roberts, and I'm
a product manager here at Posit.

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:10.500
And one of the most common
questions I get asked

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.660
especially by IT administrators and
security teams is how to

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:17.860
make sure the public packages
that their data scientists want

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:18.975
to use are safe.

00:01:19.535 --> 00:01:22.335
So today, we're going
to explore that topic.

00:01:22.335 --> 00:01:25.990
And we're gonna start with
an overview of the main public

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.010
repositories used by data scientists
working in R and Python

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:32.950
to understand how they
work, and more importantly,

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.635
how they differ in
their approaches to package publishing.

00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:40.775
We'll also explore some of
the more common types of security

00:01:40.775 --> 00:01:42.555
risks in using public packages.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:46.340
And we'll look at strategies
to mitigate those risks using

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.040
tools like Posit
Package Manager.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.935
So let's get started. And,
let's take a look at the main

00:01:52.935 --> 00:01:55.195
public package repositories
for data science.

00:01:57.015 --> 00:02:01.755
CRAN is the primary
repository for R packages.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:05.160
Bioconductor is also
widely used for R packages,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.580
primarily in data
science, life sciences.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.505
And on the Python side, the
Python package index or PI PI.

00:02:15.245 --> 00:02:18.550
Cran and biogonductor take
a similar approach to packages,

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.830
And if you've ever submitted
a package to be published on

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.370
CRAN, you know exactly
how stringent those requirements are.

00:02:26.105 --> 00:02:29.225
Each package has to pass a
comprehensive set of checks to

00:02:29.225 --> 00:02:31.885
ensure the package is properly
formed and documented,

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:36.160
builds properly, doesn't cause
any other packages to fail.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.260
Most importantly, from
a security standpoint,

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:41.875
these packages must be
submitted in entirely source

00:02:41.875 --> 00:02:44.515
code so there's full
transparency of what is

00:02:44.515 --> 00:02:46.195
contained within the package,

00:02:46.195 --> 00:02:50.020
and it will it will go
through it a actual human

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.340
verification before
being published.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.220
It's like the exclusive club
with the bouncer out front

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.585
where they may not make
sure you can dance,

00:02:57.585 --> 00:02:59.985
but they definitely are gonna
make sure you're well dressed

00:02:59.985 --> 00:03:01.285
before letting you in.

00:03:02.465 --> 00:03:07.290
In contrast, PyPI takes the
opposite approach. PyPI is much

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:08.570
more like Wikipedia,

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.770
a completely open repository
where anyone can create an

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:13.150
account and publish packages.

00:03:14.215 --> 00:03:16.795
Both in source or
binary packages,

00:03:18.055 --> 00:03:21.335
which then become instantly
available to everyone around

00:03:21.335 --> 00:03:25.150
the world. This isn't
necessarily a bad thing as it

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.150
does make a far wider array
of packages available,

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:32.005
though it does pose some
additional security risk we'll

00:03:32.005 --> 00:03:33.625
have to take a closer look at.

00:03:35.285 --> 00:03:37.525
So let's start with
the security risks now.

00:03:37.525 --> 00:03:41.570
And the first security risk
is around the more fundamental

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:43.110
question of package quality,

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:48.710
or does this package accurately
do what it claims to do?

00:03:49.255 --> 00:03:51.815
And that's not always an
easy question to answer,

00:03:51.815 --> 00:03:53.975
especially if you're
not a domain expert,

00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:56.695
but there are some other
more objective factors you can look

00:03:56.695 --> 00:03:58.075
at to help you assess quality.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.640
Things like the number of
package downloads or how many

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.180
other people are
using this package.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:07.015
The author's reputation is this
package author well known in

00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:09.655
the community? Do they have
a lot of other well used

00:04:09.655 --> 00:04:11.675
packages, that
they've published?

00:04:13.575 --> 00:04:15.290
Is this package
well documented.

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:17.130
Are there the
functions documented?

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:19.290
Are there instructional
guides, vignettes,

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.830
or other tutorials available?

00:04:22.605 --> 00:04:25.965
And how frequently is
the package updated?

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.165
When was the last update?

00:04:27.165 --> 00:04:29.485
Is this is it actively
maintained and frequently

00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:30.545
updated with fixes?

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:33.260
And finally, I mean,

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:36.620
does the package include a
comprehensive set of tests to

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.960
verify that the functionality
is working correctly?

00:04:41.465 --> 00:04:44.905
So how do we mitigate package
quality concerns? Well,

00:04:44.905 --> 00:04:48.105
the obvious option is for
someone to carefully scrutinize

00:04:48.105 --> 00:04:49.725
the package and make
a determination.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.950
But as we pointed out earlier,
that's not always easy.

00:04:54.730 --> 00:04:55.530
Fortunately,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:58.410
the community has developed some
tools to help with those

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.225
assessments. For example,

00:05:00.225 --> 00:05:03.185
our friends over at the R
validation hub have produced an

00:05:03.185 --> 00:05:06.865
R package called Risk Metric
that takes these that I just

00:05:06.865 --> 00:05:10.460
talked about and many more
quality Metrics into account to

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:14.080
help you get an objective
assessment of package quality.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.845
And once you've done your
reviews and done your assessments,

00:05:17.845 --> 00:05:20.485
and decide in which packages
you want to allow your data

00:05:20.485 --> 00:05:21.925
scientists to use,

00:05:21.925 --> 00:05:24.885
you can leverage a feature
in positive package manager called

00:05:24.885 --> 00:05:25.865
curated repositories.

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:29.630
Allowing you to create
a separate repository just

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:33.390
containing the CRAN or
PyPI packages that you've approved

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:35.010
for use in your team
or organization.

00:05:37.805 --> 00:05:38.365
So next,

00:05:38.365 --> 00:05:42.045
let's talk about what you
typically think of when you say

00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:43.665
security, and that's
vulnerabilities.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:47.060
And these can occur at
any point in the package

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.505
from the source code,
introduced in compiled code or

00:05:52.505 --> 00:05:55.065
vulnerabilities that exist in
external libraries that are

00:05:55.065 --> 00:05:56.205
used by the package.

00:05:57.145 --> 00:06:00.205
And it's important to note
that while these are sometimes malicious,

00:06:00.825 --> 00:06:02.620
most often,

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.440
these are just due to mistakes
in poorly written code.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:09.900
But they're still just as
important to to assess and resolve.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.375
So mitigating vulnerabilities is
actually a little bit easier

00:06:14.375 --> 00:06:15.335
for public packages,

00:06:15.335 --> 00:06:18.830
and that's because the
packages are known published quantities.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.150
You don't generally need
to worry about scanning them

00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:23.630
yourself because numerous
others are scanning these

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:28.130
already published packages and
reporting any vulnerabilities on them.

00:06:28.725 --> 00:06:31.605
So the best resource for
mitigating those are through

00:06:31.605 --> 00:06:33.305
databases of known
vulnerabilities.

00:06:34.965 --> 00:06:38.790
In CRANs case, are
relatively few vulnerabilities reported,

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:41.510
partially due to that strong
curation of packages due to the

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:42.730
bouncer of the door.

00:06:43.245 --> 00:06:46.045
But Posit has recently started
working with the R consortium

00:06:46.045 --> 00:06:49.085
on building out an advisory
database to track known

00:06:49.085 --> 00:06:51.880
vulnerabilities in a
standardized format that was

00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.160
first adopted by the
widely used Python packaging advisory

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.380
database that's maintained by
the Python Packaging authority.

00:06:59.555 --> 00:07:04.455
Both of these now feed into
the OSV open source vulnerability database

00:07:05.235 --> 00:07:08.455
that was, created by Google and

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:11.700
and is the best source
for looking up known

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.920
vulnerabilities in both r and
Python packages at this point.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.815
Once you have that, You
can leverage a feature in package

00:07:20.815 --> 00:07:23.235
manager called
package blocking.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.630
Where instead of taking a
bottom up curated approach to

00:07:28.630 --> 00:07:31.190
approving every package
you want to make available,

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:34.875
you can instead go top down
and allow the majority of packages

00:07:34.875 --> 00:07:36.575
available in the
public repositories,

00:07:37.115 --> 00:07:40.715
giving your users the most
compatibility with everything

00:07:40.715 --> 00:07:41.570
that's out there,

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:44.230
but still blocking the ones
that have known vulnerabilities

00:07:45.010 --> 00:07:49.625
and even for blocking packages
that may have, license open

00:07:49.625 --> 00:07:52.185
source licenses that you
don't wanna make available inside

00:07:52.185 --> 00:07:52.845
your organization.

00:07:55.065 --> 00:07:55.705
Now,

00:07:55.705 --> 00:07:58.790
separate from the security risk
in the packages themselves,

00:07:58.790 --> 00:08:01.430
there are a couple more
malicious risks that prey on

00:08:01.430 --> 00:08:03.910
weaknesses in the
infrastructure that is used to

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:05.050
distribute these packages.

00:08:05.915 --> 00:08:09.355
We group these into a category
called package confusion,

00:08:09.355 --> 00:08:13.850
which in simplest terms is
all about deceiving the user into

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:15.930
installing the wrong package.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:18.810
So instead of getting the
package they want to use,

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:21.950
they are tricked into installing
a malicious package that they don't.

00:08:22.475 --> 00:08:26.235
The two risks we'll talk about
today are type o squatting and

00:08:26.235 --> 00:08:27.195
dependency confusion,

00:08:27.195 --> 00:08:29.595
which are some of the
more common ones and most high

00:08:29.595 --> 00:08:31.950
profile ones that we've
seen in the last year.

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:37.470
So I'm going going to start
and give some examples in terms of

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:42.015
Python packages as due to those
that more permissive nature of PyPI.

00:08:42.015 --> 00:08:45.135
These have shown up in
high profile real world exploits in

00:08:45.135 --> 00:08:46.195
the past year.

00:08:46.815 --> 00:08:50.755
But the same cases can apply
to the r package repositories as well.

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:54.190
So first, let's learn
about type of squatting.

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.730
And let's start with you,
the data scientist or developer.

00:09:00.675 --> 00:09:05.655
And, we're sitting at
our local machine, and you,

00:09:07.155 --> 00:09:10.210
let's say we wanna
start a machine learning project.

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:13.990
And so you want to
grab your TensorFlow package from PyPI.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:18.225
You use the standard
Python installation utility PyPI and

00:09:18.225 --> 00:09:20.305
say PIP install TensorFlow,

00:09:20.305 --> 00:09:22.865
which is the command
to install TensorFlow,

00:09:22.865 --> 00:09:26.465
equivalent to doing an install dot
packages and r to install a

00:09:26.465 --> 00:09:28.330
package from the from prem.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:32.310
And so PIP goes out to PyPI.

00:09:32.310 --> 00:09:35.145
Looks for a package
called TensorFlow. Downloads it,

00:09:35.145 --> 00:09:37.705
installs it into
your environment. Perfect.

00:09:37.705 --> 00:09:38.685
Everything's fine.

00:09:39.945 --> 00:09:43.805
But now let's, start again
back in our clean environment.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:48.670
We've still got PIPI there,
but this time, when we try to

00:09:48.670 --> 00:09:49.630
install TensorFlow,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:53.325
we accidentally make a typo and had
two n's in the name of the

00:09:53.325 --> 00:09:57.665
package. So pip so we have
PIP install TensorFlow with two n's.

00:09:58.525 --> 00:10:01.350
PIP goes out and looks for
it on PIPI and can't find a

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:05.350
package by that name and
returns an error. You notice

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.230
you mistype the name, fix it,
and try again? All as well.

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:10.405
That's what you expect
to happen in those situations,

00:10:10.405 --> 00:10:12.985
and nothing inherently
wrong with that.

00:10:13.525 --> 00:10:17.445
But here's where things get scary.
So let's go back to our

00:10:17.445 --> 00:10:18.720
our clean environment here.

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.700
And let's say that
some evil person

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.080
try up up to no good looks
at the most popular public

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:29.955
packages on PyPI. Like
TensorFlow, which, for example,

00:10:29.955 --> 00:10:33.315
gets downloaded from PIPI
on average about six hundred

00:10:33.315 --> 00:10:35.175
thousand times a day globally.

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:38.990
And so they take that and
they write their own malicious

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:43.330
package and upload it to PyPIwith
a bunch of different similar names,

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:48.915
that are common things that you
might make type by accident if

00:10:48.915 --> 00:10:51.155
you were make if you were
trying to install the actual

00:10:51.155 --> 00:10:52.215
TensorFlow package,

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:54.760
So now we let's take
our same scenario.

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:58.280
We try to install TensorFlow with
two n's in it and make the

00:10:58.280 --> 00:10:59.560
same type it was before,

00:10:59.560 --> 00:11:01.320
but instead of getting
an error this time,

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.025
PIP goes out and to PyPI
and finds a malicious this

00:11:04.025 --> 00:11:05.965
malicious person that
someone put there.

00:11:06.585 --> 00:11:08.825
And with the same
name, downloads it,

00:11:08.825 --> 00:11:12.770
and we've been exploited now because
we've gotten the wrong packaging here.

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.125
So these are tough
to mitigate against.

00:11:17.125 --> 00:11:19.845
But there are a few
things that we can do.

00:11:19.845 --> 00:11:22.725
And I should point out again
that these are higher risk on

00:11:22.725 --> 00:11:25.685
with PyPI than on CRAN
due to the more permissive publishing

00:11:25.685 --> 00:11:26.905
nature we talked about.

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:30.630
But one thing working to our
advantages in that case is that

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.010
there are many organizations

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:36.075
and companies in the
Python community watching for these

00:11:36.075 --> 00:11:37.595
malicious packages now.

00:11:37.595 --> 00:11:39.995
So they're identified
relatively quickly once they

00:11:39.995 --> 00:11:42.895
are published, usually
within a few days or less.

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:48.070
So the simplest mitigation
we can do is just avoid the

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.500
latest packages and updates
from public repositories

00:11:51.175 --> 00:11:53.415
and give them time to
be discovered, reported,

00:11:53.415 --> 00:11:54.715
and blocked or removed.

00:11:55.335 --> 00:11:58.580
And so, you know, latest
isn't always the safest there.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:01.620
And sometimes it's worth it to
sacrifice a bit of cutting edge

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:02.520
and com compatibility,

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.800
for for in the
name of security.

00:12:06.995 --> 00:12:09.875
So in package manager,
we can leverage,

00:12:09.875 --> 00:12:13.095
what we call repository snapshots
to easily pin our installation

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.920
source to a slightly older
versions of the repositories.

00:12:18.100 --> 00:12:21.460
We we also get the added
benefit of reproducibility of

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.405
our projects by not
always getting updates the packages

00:12:24.405 --> 00:12:26.345
that might break
our existing code.

00:12:27.045 --> 00:12:28.565
And, you know, it's
not a perfect solution,

00:12:28.565 --> 00:12:30.805
but it's one tool that can
be used together with these other

00:12:30.805 --> 00:12:33.305
strategies we're talking
about to reduce our risks.

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:36.230
Finally,

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:41.395
I wanna talk about a different
variant that prays particularly on larger teams,

00:12:41.395 --> 00:12:44.435
especially those who develop
and share their own internal

00:12:44.435 --> 00:12:46.275
packages to
supplement their work,

00:12:46.275 --> 00:12:47.815
then we call
dependency confusion.

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.290
So let's go back into
our clean environment here where we're

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:54.830
now part of a larger company
that has its own internal packages.

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:56.855
Let's say we're at Posit,

00:12:56.855 --> 00:13:00.935
and we have our internal Posit
Tools package that we use along

00:13:00.935 --> 00:13:02.795
with some public
packages from PyPI.

00:13:03.495 --> 00:13:06.650
We may even be so sophisticated
as to have our own internal

00:13:06.650 --> 00:13:09.150
packages and a PyPI like, repository

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:11.950
so that PIP can
install from there.

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:17.085
So we we wanna install POSIT
Tools. We add this extra index

00:13:17.085 --> 00:13:19.985
URL flag to our
internal server.

00:13:20.925 --> 00:13:25.260
So so that it can find
where our additional packages are. In reality,

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:28.915
this is pro this extra index
URL setting is probably set as

00:13:28.915 --> 00:13:30.355
part of our system
configuration,

00:13:30.355 --> 00:13:34.215
so we don't have to remember to
type it every time we install something.

00:13:34.835 --> 00:13:37.715
But I include this here because
this is exactly what would

00:13:37.715 --> 00:13:39.480
actually happen in reality.

00:13:40.580 --> 00:13:41.940
So let's say PIP goes out,

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:45.540
looks for a positive tools
package on PyPI, doesn't find

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:47.205
